{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 40, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-397b-a17b", "content": "the social network is a masterful film directed by david fincher that chronicles the founding of facebook and the subsequent legal battles involving its creator, mark zuckerberg. the script, written by aaron sorkin, is sharp, fast-paced, and filled with intelligent dialogue that drives the narrative forward with incredible momentum. jesse eisenberg delivers a standout performance as zuckerberg, portraying him as a socially awkward yet brilliant and somewhat ruthless genius whose ambition alienates those closest to him. andrew garfield also shines as eduardo saverin, the co-founder whose friendship with zuckerberg deteriorates as the company grows. the cinematography by jeff cronenweth uses cool tones and precise framing to reflect the cold, calculated nature of the tech world and the emotional isolation of the characters. trent reznor and atticus ross composed an electronic score that is haunting and atmospheric, perfectly complementing the film's tense mood. unlike many biopics, this movie does not simply celebrate success; it dissects the cost of ambition, betrayal, and the complexity of human relationships in the digital age. the non-linear storytelling structure keeps the audience engaged as it weaves together the creation of the site with two separate lawsuits. ultimately, the social network is not just a story about a website; it is a modern tragedy about loneliness, envy, and the desire for validation. it remains one of the most compelling films of the twenty-first century, offering a timeless look at how technology can connect millions while driving individuals apart.", "logprobs": [{"token": "the", "logprob": -0.34322602}, {"token": " social", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " network", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -0.15502208}, {"token": " a", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " master", "logprob": -2.4338179}, {"token": "ful", "logprob": -0